---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { dead } from "../lib/people.js";
import places from "../data/places.json";
import reads from "../data/scilla-reads.json";
import m13 from "../data/scilla-matr-1913.json";
const here = dead.filter((p) => [p.birthPlace, p.deathPlace, p.burialPlace]
  .some((x) => (x || "").startsWith("Scilla")));
const pl = places.find((x) => x.name.startsWith("Scilla")) || {};
const surnames = [...new Set(here.map((p) => p.surname).filter(Boolean))].sort();
---
<Base title="Scilla" eyebrow="Reggio Calabria"
      dek="The rock at the northern gate of the Strait of Messina, and the fishing quarter beneath it. Two of the four grandparents were born here, four years apart, and did not marry until Brisbane.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{here.length}</div><div class="l">people recorded at Scilla</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{pl.first || "1821"}</div><div class="l">the earliest birth named here</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">2</div><div class="l">of the four quarters begin here</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The place</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Scilla is the <em>Scylla</em> of the <em>Odyssey</em> — the rock opposite the whirlpool of
      Charybdis, at the narrowest point of the strait that separates Calabria from Sicily. The
      <strong>Castello Ruffo</strong> sits on the headland; below it, on the water, is
      <strong>Chianalea</strong>, a lane of houses built straight into the sea where the boats are
      moored at the doors. This was a swordfishing town, and it still is.</p>
      <p>The geography is the point for this family. From Chianalea you can see
      <a href={u("/piedimonte-etneo/")}>Etna</a> on a clear morning. The Scilla families and the
      Piedimonte Etneo family were looking at each other across thirty kilometres of water for
      generations — and met on the other side of the world.</p>
      <p>Scilla was levelled by the <strong>1783 earthquake</strong> and the tsunami that followed it,
      when a wave took a large part of the population off the beach at Marina Grande. As at
      <a href={u("/briatico/")}>Briatico</a>, that is why the record here begins where it does.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The families</h2>
    <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;line-height:2;margin-top:.8rem">
      {surnames.map((s, i) => <>{i > 0 && " · "}<strong>{s}</strong></>)}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The registers, opened 10 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">An index that names the parents — and misleads</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Scilla is <strong>not</strong> in FamilySearch's full-text corpus — four searches
      place-matched to it returned nothing, and its Calabrian holdings are Catanzaro and Vibo
      Valentia. But <strong>Antenati has 57 volumes</strong> from the Archivio di Stato di Reggio
      Calabria, births from <strong>1850</strong>; marriages run <strong>1905–1913 only</strong>.</p>
      <p>The pre-unification <em>Stato civile della restaurazione</em> index is laid out far better
      than the Italian one that replaced it. Its columns are <strong>N. d'ord · Cognomi e Nomi dei
      nati · Patria · Professione · NOMI E COGNOMI DEI GENITORI · giorno della nascita</strong>.
      <strong>The index names the parents</strong> — a whole year's households of one surname read off
      a single opening.</p>
      <p><strong>And it will mislead you if you trust it.</strong> See below.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 228 of 1861</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Rocco Arena, born 20 December 1861</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/scilla-act228-arena-rocco.jpg")} alt="Scilla birth register 1861, act 228" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">L'anno mille ottocento sessant<strong>uno</strong> il dì
        <strong>ventitre</strong> del mese di <strong>Dicembre</strong>… Comune di <strong>Scilla</strong>,
        Distretto di <strong>Reggio</strong>, Provincia della Prima Calabria Ulteriore, è comparso
        <strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong>, <strong>figli di Filippo</strong>, di anni
        <strong>quarantadue</strong>, di professione <strong>marinaro</strong>, domiciliato in Scilla…
        è nat[o] da <strong>Serafina Arlotta</strong>, di anni <strong>ventotto</strong>… nel giorno
        <strong>venti</strong> del mese di Dicembre… il nome di <strong>Rocco</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p><strong>Three generations in one act:</strong> <strong>Filippo Arena</strong>, his son
        <strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong> — born about 1819, and a <em>marinaro</em>, a sailor —
        and the boy <strong>Rocco</strong>. Witnessed by Giuseppe Idone, 64, and Francesco Antonio
        Polì, 70, <em>proprietario</em>; the parish note records the baptism on the 23rd.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
        della restaurazione, Scilla, Nati 1861, atto 228 — Antenati <code>an_ua2002933</code>, image 117.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 206 of 1883</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Giovanni Polistena, born 16 September 1883</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/scilla-act206-polistena-giovanni.jpg")} alt="Scilla birth register 1883, act 206" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">L'anno milleottocento ottant<strong>atre</strong>, addì
        <strong>diciassette</strong> di <strong>Settembre</strong>… Uffiziale dello Stato Civile del
        Comune di <strong>Scilla</strong>, è comparso <strong>Camillo Antonio Polistena</strong>, di
        anni <strong>quarantotto</strong>, <strong>pescatore</strong>, domiciliato in questo Comune…
        nella casa posta in <strong>Via Grotte</strong> al numero <strong>quarantasette</strong>, da
        <strong>Giovanna Romano</strong> sua moglie, <strong>filatrice</strong>, seco lui
        convivente… a cui dà il nome di <strong>Giovanni</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p><P n="Angela Polistena" />'s father, born at half past nine at night in a house on
        <strong>Via Grotte</strong> — the lane of houses standing in the water at
        <strong>Chianalea</strong>. His own father <strong>Camillo Antonio Polistena</strong> was
        forty-eight, so born about <strong>1835</strong>, and a <strong>fisherman</strong>.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem"><strong>The father agrees with the family tree</strong>, which
        calls him Antonio Polistena. <strong>The mother does not.</strong> The register says
        <strong>Giovanna Romano</strong>; the tree says <strong>Giovanna Pontillo</strong> — and gives
        that Giovanna two parents <em>both</em> surnamed Pontillo, which reads like an error in the
        tree rather than a second marriage.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
        italiano, Scilla, Nati 1883, atto 206 — Antenati <code>an_ua16601246</code>, image 73.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 230 of 1892</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Giuseppe Arena, born 6 October 1892 — and his father is eleven years older than the tree says</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/scilla-act230-arena-giuseppe-1892.jpg")} alt="Scilla birth register 1892, act 230" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">L'anno mille-ottocento-novant<strong>adue</strong>, addì
        <strong>nove</strong> di <strong>Ottobre</strong>… è comparso <strong>Giovanni Arena</strong>,
        di anni <strong>trentatré</strong>, <strong>pescatore</strong>, domiciliato in
        <strong>Scilla</strong>, il quale mi ha dichiarato che alle ore ante meridiane
        <strong>undici</strong> e minuti <strong>trenta</strong> del dì <strong>sei</strong> del
        corrente mese, nella casa posta in <strong>Via Grotte</strong> al numero
        <strong>settantacinque</strong>, da <strong>Concetta Russo</strong> sua moglie,
        <strong>filatrice</strong>, seco lui convivente… è nato un bambino di sesso maschile… a cui
        dà il nome di <strong>Giuseppe</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p>This is the most useful act the Scilla sweep has produced. It is
        <P n="Rocco Arena" />'s father, and it <strong>confirms the tree on two points and corrects
        it on a third</strong>.</p>
        <div class="rec" style="margin-top:14px">
          <div class="k">Confirmed</div>
          <div class="v"><strong>Giuseppe Arena</strong>, born at Scilla in <strong>1892</strong> —
          but on <strong>6 October</strong>, not 10 June as the tree has it</div>
          <div class="w">year agrees</div>
          <div class="k">Confirmed</div>
          <div class="v">his mother <strong>Concetta Russo</strong>, a <em>filatrice</em> — a
          spinner</div><div class="w">agrees</div>
          <div class="k">Corrected</div>
          <div class="v"><strong>Giovanni Arena is thirty-three</strong> in October 1892, so he was
          born about <strong>1859</strong>. The tree says <strong>1870</strong>.</div>
          <div class="w">eleven years</div>
          <div class="k">Corrected</div>
          <div class="v">Giuseppe's own birth date. The tree says <strong>10 June 1892</strong>; the
          register says he was born at <strong>noon-ish on 6 October</strong> and declared on the
          9th. The 1892 index holds <strong>only one</strong> Arena Giuseppe, and his mother is
          Concetta Russo, so this is the same child.</div>
          <div class="w">four months</div>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">It also gives Giovanni something the tree never did: a trade.
        He is a <strong>pescatore</strong>, a fisherman — and the house is on
        <strong>Via Grotte</strong>, the same lane at Chianalea where
        <a href={u("/people/giovanni-polistena/")}>Giovanni Polistena</a> was born nine years earlier
        at number 47. Two of this family's four Calabrian quarters begin in the same street.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
        italiano, Scilla, Nati 1892, atto 230 — Antenati <code>an_ua16601257</code>, container
        <code>5Yb6no7</code>, image 79.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 22 of 1893</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Anna Arena, born 17 January 1893 — and the midwife who declared her</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/scilla-act22-arena-anna-1893.jpg")} alt="Scilla birth register 1893, act 22" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">…è comparsa <strong>Giovanna Zumbo</strong>, di anni
        <strong>sessanta</strong>, <strong>levatrice</strong>, domiciliata in Scilla, la quale mi ha
        dichiarato che alle ore ante meridiane <strong>quattro</strong> e minuti
        <strong>quindici</strong>, del dì <strong>diciassette</strong> del corrente mese, nella casa
        posta in <strong>Via Acquagrande</strong> al numero <strong>quarantuno</strong>, da
        <strong>Costa Generosa</strong>, <strong>filatrice</strong>, moglie di <strong>Arena Rocco,
        marinaro</strong>, ambedue domiciliati in Scilla… a cui dà il nome di
        <strong>Anna</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p>This act was opened to do to <P n="Rocco Arena" /> what act 230 did to Giovanni Arena —
        pin his birth year from his child's act. <strong>It does not, and the reason is in the first
        line.</strong> The declarant is not the father but <strong>Giovanna Zumbo, sixty, the town
        midwife</strong>, and a midwife's declaration names the parents without their ages.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem">What it does give is worth having. The date and the mother
        <strong>Generosa Costa</strong> match the tree exactly. And Rocco Arena gets a trade:
        <strong><em>marinaro</em>, a sailor</strong>.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem"><strong>That trade cuts against this archive's own working
        hypothesis, so it is recorded plainly.</strong> The Rocco Arena of act 228 of 1861 — the one
        whose parents are Francesco Antonio Arena and Serafina Arlotta rather than the tree's Orazio
        and Giovanna Zirilli — is the son of a <em>marinaro</em>, and these trades passed from father
        to son. It is weak evidence. It points the other way from
        <strong>act 46 of 1859</strong> below, and the question stays open rather than being
        quietly resolved in the direction the archive was leaning.</p>
        <div class="rec" style="margin-top:14px">
          <div class="k">Via Grotte</div>
          <div class="v"><strong>Giovanni Arena</strong>, <em>pescatore</em>, at no. 75 —
          <P n="Giuseppe Arena" />'s household</div><div class="w">1892</div>
          <div class="k">Via Acquagrande</div>
          <div class="v"><strong>Rocco Arena</strong>, <em>marinaro</em>, at no. 41 —
          <P n="Anna Arena" />'s household</div><div class="w">1893</div>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>Two Arena families, in two different quarters of the same
        small town</strong> — and a year apart they each had a child who would marry the other.
        Giuseppe and Anna are both great-grandparents here.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
        italiano, Scilla, Nati 1893, atto 22 — Antenati <code>an_ua16601258</code>, container
        <code>w9WVxa9</code>, image 9. The ark was <em>computed</em>, not looked up, from the rule
        recorded in the method notes, and the volume's title page confirmed it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 162 of 1892</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Annunziata Donato, born 6 July 1892 — and why 1851 was empty</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/scilla-act162-donato-annunziata-1892.jpg")} alt="Scilla birth register 1892, act 162" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">…è comparso <strong>Michele Donato</strong>, di anni
        <strong>trentasei</strong>, <strong>pescatore</strong>, domiciliato in <strong>Scilla</strong>…
        alle ore pomeridiane <strong>quattro</strong> e minuti <strong>trenta</strong> del dì
        <strong>sei</strong> del corrente mese, nella casa posta in <strong>Via Grotte</strong> al
        numero <strong>trentuno</strong>, da <strong>Maria Sofi</strong> sua moglie,
        <strong>filatrice</strong>… a cui dà i nomi di <strong>Annunziata</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p><strong>This cost nothing.</strong> The 1892 volume was already open for act 230, and a
        child's birth act names its father <em>with an age</em> — so the fastest route to a man who
        cannot be found is often his children, not him.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem"><strong>Michele Donato is thirty-six in July 1892</strong>, so
        born about <strong>1856</strong>. That is why the 1851 index below had no Donato in it: the
        tree's year is five years out, and the archive was reading the wrong volume.
        <P n="Maria Sofi" /> is confirmed exactly as the tree has her, and he gains a trade the tree
        never gave him — <strong>pescatore</strong>.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem">The witnesses are both fishermen too: <strong>Arlotta
        Giuseppe</strong>, 56, and <strong>Ponte Antonio</strong>, 38. <em>Arlotta</em> is the
        surname of Rocco Arena's mother in act 228 of 1861 — thirty-one years and two families
        apart, on the same few hundred metres of shore.</p>
        <div class="rec" style="margin-top:14px">
          <div class="k">New</div>
          <div class="v">The marginal annotation records a death <strong>the tree does not have at
          all</strong>: <strong><P n="Nunnziata Donato" /> died at Scilla on 26 August 1975</strong>,
          death act 30 of that year. She was 83.</div><div class="w">atto 30 / 1975</div>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">That last fact rearranges the family's shape a little. Her
        daughters <P n="Angela Polistena" /> and <P n="Giovanna Polistena" /> are both in the
        <a href={u("/nudgee/")}>Nudgee burial register</a> in Brisbane. <strong>Their mother never
        left.</strong> She was still at Scilla half a century after they sailed.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
        italiano, Scilla, Nati 1892, atto 162 — Antenati <code>an_ua16601257</code>, container
        <code>5Yb6no7</code>, image 56.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 81 of 1870</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The Arena boy who was not the one we wanted</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/scilla-act81-arena-1870.jpg")} alt="Scilla birth register 1870, act 81" />
      <div>
        <p>Before act 230 was found, the obvious way to reach Giovanni Arena was to open
        <strong>Scilla, Nati 1870</strong> and look for him. The annual index gave three Arena births
        that year, and exactly one with <em>Giovanni</em> among his names:</p>
        <blockquote style="margin:.9rem 0">…è comparso <strong>Giuseppe Arena di Domenico</strong>, di
        anni <strong>trenta</strong>, <strong>cordaio</strong>, domiciliato e residente in Scilla…
        dalla di lui moglie <strong>Serafina Vita di Giovanni</strong>, di anni <strong>venti</strong>,
        donna di casa… in questo Comune, <strong>Centro Acquagrande</strong>, al quale figlio dichiara
        dare i nomi di <strong>Domenico-Giovanni-Camillo</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p>Three Arena generations in one act — <strong>Domenico</strong>, his son
        <strong>Giuseppe</strong> born about 1840, and the boy. And it was tempting: the tree's
        Giovanni Arena named his own first son <strong>Giuseppe</strong>, which is exactly what
        Calabrian naming custom would predict if his father were a Giuseppe.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem"><strong>It is the wrong man, and three things say so.</strong>
        The marginal annotation records that he married at Scilla on <strong>30 November 1899</strong>
        to <strong>Arena Maria</strong>, born there in 1869 — not to Concetta Russo. The annotation
        calls him <strong>Arena Domenico</strong>, not Giovanni, which the naming custom also
        predicts: Domenico was his father's father, Giovanni only his mother's. And act 230 then
        settled it outright by making the tree's Giovanni eleven years older than this child.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem">His father's trade is worth keeping too. <strong>Giuseppe Arena
        was a <em>cordaio</em>, a ropemaker</strong> — the first man in the Calabrian lines this
        archive has found who does not work the water, though rope is not far from boats.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
        italiano, Scilla, Nati 1870, atto 81 — Antenati <code>an_ua16601232</code>, container
        <code>wWqVnPB</code>, image 19. The mother's surname <strong>Vita</strong> is a real Scilla
        surname, not a misreading of <em>Sita</em>: it appears three times in the 1913 marriage index
        below, and <em>Sita</em> never.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">What the registers keep saying</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">They were sea families</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Every man the registers have named in this archive's Calabrian lines works the water:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:14px">
        <div class="k">Scilla, 1861</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong> — <em>marinaro</em></div><div class="w">sailor</div>
        <div class="k">Scilla, 1883</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Camillo Antonio Polistena</strong> — <em>pescatore</em></div><div class="w">fisherman</div>
        <div class="k">Scilla, 1892</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Giovanni Arena</strong> — <em>pescatore</em></div><div class="w">fisherman</div>
        <div class="k">Briatico, 1886</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Alfonso Prostamo</strong> and his father <strong>Domenico</strong> — <em>marinari</em></div><div class="w">sailors</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">There is now <strong>one exception</strong>, and it is worth
      naming rather than hiding: <strong>Giuseppe Arena</strong>, in 1870, is a <em>cordaio</em> —
      a ropemaker. He is a different Arena household from the tree's, and rope is a harbour trade,
      but he is not on the water. A pattern that admits an exception is a stronger claim than one
      that has never met one.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>The family tree records not one of them as anything.</strong>
      Two provinces, three families, and the same trade — which is exactly what you would expect of
      Chianalea and the Costa degli Dei, and exactly what an undated pedigree cannot tell you.
      The Sicilian <a href={u("/piedimonte-etneo/")}>Mazza</a>, up on the mountain, are
      <em>braccianti</em> and <em>contadini</em> to a man. <strong>The archive can now say what these
      people did, which it could not this morning.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Scilla, Nati 1819 — act 186</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Two generations the family tree does not have</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Once act 45 of 1860 had moved <P n="Rocco Arena" /> from Orazio Arena to <strong>Francesco
      Antonio Arena</strong>, a door opened that had been shut. Act 228 of 1861 gives Francesco
      Antonio an age — <strong>forty-two</strong> — and calls him, in as many words,
      <em>figlio di Filippo</em>. Forty-two in December 1861 means born about 1819. The
      <strong>Tavola annuale alfabetica de' nati in questo Comune di Scilla nell'anno 1819</strong>
      has an A section of five entries, and the fifth is this:</p>
      <blockquote style="margin:1.1rem 0;font-size:17px"><strong>5 · Arena Francesco ·
      Filippo e Dom.<sup>ca</sup> Costa · 25 9<sup>bre</sup> · 186</strong></blockquote>
      <p><strong>Born 25 November 1819, he was forty-two years and one month on the day his son
      Rocco was declared.</strong> The fit is exact, and it hands the archive two people at once:
      <strong>Filippo Arena</strong> and <strong>Domenica Costa</strong>.</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:18px">
        <div class="k">gen 7</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Filippo Arena</strong> × <strong>Domenica Costa</strong> — married by
        1819</div><div class="w">not in the tree</div>
        <div class="k">gen 6</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong>, b. <strong>25 November 1819</strong>,
        <em>marinaro</em> × <strong>Serafina Arlotta</strong>, b. c.1833</div>
        <div class="w">not in the tree</div>
        <div class="k">gen 5</div>
        <div class="v"><P n="Rocco Arena" />, b. 20 December 1861, <em>marinaro</em> ×
        <P n="Generosa Costa" /></div><div class="w">in the tree</div>
        <div class="k">gen 4</div>
        <div class="v"><P n="Anna Arena" />, b. 17 January 1893</div><div class="w">in the tree</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>Four people in that table are not in Michael's export at
      all</strong>, and two of them are great-great-great-great-grandparents of Mia and Rocco. They
      cannot be drawn on this site's charts, because the charts draw the tree as it was given —
      so they are listed on <a href={u("/corrections/")}>For Michael</a> instead.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><strong>A surname that keeps coming back.</strong>
      Francesco Antonio's mother is a <strong>Costa</strong>. Three generations later his grandson
      Rocco married <strong>Generosa Costa</strong>. On present evidence that is a coincidence of a
      common Scilla surname and nothing more — but it is the kind of thing worth writing down before
      it is needed, and the 1901 <em>Neustria</em> carried a Costa and a Donato of Scilla two lines
      apart.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
      della restaurazione, Scilla, Nati 1819 — Antenati <code>an_ua2002808</code>, container
      <code>58rKlpd</code>, Tavola at images 115–121.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Scilla, Nati 1830 — atto 2</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Serafina Arlotta, born 13 June 1830</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The marriage act of 1851 gave <strong>Serafina Arlotta</strong> parents — Giovanni Arlotta,
      <em>marinaro</em>, and Maria Costa — and an age, twenty. The 1830 Tavola gives her a date and
      confirms the parents from a second, independent document:</p>
      <blockquote style="margin:1.1rem 0;font-size:17px"><strong>2 · Arlotta Serafina ·
      Giovanni, e Costa Maria · 13 Giugno</strong></blockquote>
      <p><strong>Born 13 June 1830, she was twenty years and seven months on her wedding day.</strong>
      Exact. Rocco Arena's mother now has a birth date, and his maternal grandparents are documented
      twice over.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><strong>It also corrects an age this archive had been
      carrying.</strong> Act 228 of 1861 makes Serafina <strong>28</strong>, which would put her birth
      in 1833 — and this archive recorded that. She was <strong>31</strong>. The register's own ages
      are the loosest thing in it, which is exactly why a birth act beats an age every time.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Scilla,
      Nati 1830 — Antenati <code>an_ua2002840</code>, container <code>LaNd12r</code>, Tavola at images
      119–125.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Scilla, Matrimoni 1851 — atto 13</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Four parents in one act</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The Scilla marriage register is the most efficient document in the whole system: it names
      <strong>both spouses' parents</strong>. This is the act the archive spent three sessions failing
      to find — first in 1858, then 1859, both wrong because the couple had married years earlier
      than their children implied.</p>
      <blockquote style="margin:1.1rem 0">L'anno mille ottocento <strong>cinquantuno</strong> il dì
      <strong>sette</strong> del mese di <strong>Febbraro</strong>… sono comparsi nella Casa comunale
      <strong>Francesco Arena</strong> di anni <strong>trentuno</strong>, nato in questo Comune, di
      professione <strong>marinaro</strong>, <strong>figlio di Filippo</strong>, di professione
      <strong>stessa</strong>, e di <strong>Domenica Costa</strong>… E <strong>Serafina Arlotta</strong>
      di anni <strong>venti</strong>, nata in questo Comune, <strong>figlia di Giovanni</strong>, di
      professione <strong>marinaro</strong>, e di <strong>Maria Costa</strong>.</blockquote>
      <p><strong>Thirty-one on 7 February 1851 means born about 1819 — and act 186 of 1819 gives
      25 November 1819.</strong> Exact. The promise was made on the 7th and the church celebrated it
      on the 16th.</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:18px">
        <div class="k">Confirms</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Filippo Arena</strong> and <strong>Domenica Costa</strong>, from a
        second document independent of the 1819 index</div><div class="w">gen 7</div>
        <div class="k">Adds</div>
        <div class="v">Filippo's trade — <em>professione <strong>stessa</strong></em>, the same as his
        son's: <strong>marinaro</strong></div><div class="w">new</div>
        <div class="k">Adds</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Giovanni Arlotta</strong>, <em>marinaro</em>, and
        <strong>Maria Costa</strong> — Serafina's parents, and two people no one in this family knew
        the name of</div><div class="w">gen 7</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>Three generations of sailors, and now a fourth beside
      them.</strong> Filippo Arena, his son Francesco Antonio, his grandson Rocco — and Serafina's
      father Giovanni Arlotta. Every man this act names works the water.</p>
      <p><strong>And a third Costa.</strong> Filippo Arena married a <strong>Domenica Costa</strong>;
      Giovanni Arlotta married a <strong>Maria Costa</strong>; and three generations later their
      great-grandson Rocco married <strong>Generosa Costa</strong>. A
      <a href={u("/crossings/")}>Lorenzo Costa was lodging with Rocco in New York</a> in 1900. On the
      evidence this is a common Scilla surname recurring, and it has now recurred four times.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><strong>How this was found, and the mistake on the
      way.</strong> The marriage Tavola's number column was first read as <strong>19 and 20</strong>.
      Acts 19 and 20 were duly opened and turned out to be Bruno × Calario and Papalia × Briganti.
      The archive then wrote that the tavola must index some other series — and it was wrong. <strong>The
      numbers are 13 and 6.</strong> It had misread its own digits and blamed the document. The lesson
      is small and worth keeping: when a record does not match, check your own reading before you
      theorise about the record.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
      della restaurazione, Scilla, Matrimoni 1851, atto 13 — Antenati <code>an_ua2002614</code>,
      container <code>LD6brxB</code>, image 15; Tavola at images 88–96.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Scilla, Nati 1860 — acts 45 and 161</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The tree gave Orazio Arena another man's children</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Four days of this archive have gone on one contradiction: the tree makes
      <P n="Rocco Arena" /> the son of <P n="Orazio Arena" /> and Giovana Zirilli; act 228 of 1861
      says <strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong> and <strong>Serafina Arlotta</strong>. The 1860
      Tavola settles it, and not by arithmetic.</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:18px">
        <div class="k">The tree</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Orazio Arena</strong> has exactly <strong>two</strong> children:
        <strong>Rocco</strong> and <strong>Guiseppe</strong></div><div class="w">no source</div>
        <div class="k">atto 45 · 1860</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Arena Giuseppe</strong> — <em>Franc. Ant.o e Arlotta Serafina</em> —
        5 March 1860</div><div class="w">register</div>
        <div class="k">atto 228 · 1861</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Arena Rocco</strong> — <em>Francesco Antonio Arena, marinaro, e
        Serafina Arlotta</em> — 20 December 1861</div><div class="w">register</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>Francesco Antonio Arena and Serafina Arlotta had a
      Giuseppe and a Rocco, twenty-one months apart. The tree gives Orazio Arena a Rocco and a
      Giuseppe and nothing else.</strong> It is the same sibling pair, in the same order, hung on the
      wrong father.</p>
      <p>And Orazio's real children are now on the page. Across four years of the Tavola —
      <strong>1856, 1858, 1859 and 1860</strong> — Orazio Arena and Giovanna Zirilli produce:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
        <div class="k">1856 · atto 180</div><div class="v">Domenico, 3 September</div><div class="w"></div>
        <div class="k">1859 · atto 46</div><div class="v">Domenico, 10 February — the first boy
        having evidently died, and the name reused</div><div class="w"></div>
        <div class="k">1860 · atto 161</div><div class="v">Maria Annunziata, 27 August</div><div class="w"></div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>No Rocco. No Giuseppe.</strong> Not in any of the four
      years read. The couple is real, well documented and busy — and neither of the two children the
      tree assigns them ever appears.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>A lead that was written here and has since
      been withdrawn.</strong> This paragraph originally argued that because Orazio's first son is
      Domenico, twice, naming custom made <em>his</em> father a Domenico Arena. Two Orazio Arena birth
      acts have since turned up and neither father is a Domenico — the better candidate, matching the
      tree's own year of 1821, gives <strong>Giuseppe</strong>. The retraction and what it teaches are
      below.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">The same A section also holds <strong>Arena Domenico,
      act 43, of <em>Giovanni e Longordo Maddalena</em></strong> — a Giovanni Arena fathering a child
      in 1860, so an adult born in the 1830s, and <em>not</em> the Giovanni Arena this archive wants.
      Recorded so the two are never merged. Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile della
      restaurazione, Scilla, Nati 1860 — <code>an_ua2002931</code>, container <code>wr9rbv8</code>,
      Tavola at images 136–142.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Scilla, Nati 1859 — act 46</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Orazio Arena and Giovanna Zirilli exist after all</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The 1859 volume was opened looking for <strong>Giovanni Arena</strong>, whom act 230 of 1892
      had just relocated from 1870 to about 1859. He is not there. The A section holds exactly three
      Arena births — and the first of them is worth more than the one that was wanted:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
        <div class="k">act 46</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Arena Domenico</strong>, Scilla — <strong>Orazio e Zirilli
        Giovanna</strong> — 10 February 1859</div><div class="w">the couple</div>
        <div class="k">act 111</div>
        <div class="v">Arena Maria, Scilla — Giosafatto e Longordo Rosaria — 1 May 1859</div>
        <div class="w">not in the tree</div>
        <div class="k">act 205</div>
        <div class="v">Arena Annunziato, Scilla — Santo e Gioffrè Giovanna — 15 September 1859</div>
        <div class="w">not in the tree</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong><P n="Orazio Arena" /> and Giovanna Zirilli are the couple
      the family tree names as <P n="Rocco Arena" />'s parents</strong> — and until now this archive
      had been unable to find either of them in any register at all. The 1861 act said something else
      entirely, and the honest reading was that the tree might simply be wrong. It is not. They were
      at Scilla, and they were having children.</p>
      <p><strong>What this does and does not settle.</strong> It does not make Rocco their son: the
      1861 register contains exactly one Rocco Arena and he belongs to Francesco Antonio Arena,
      <em>marinaro</em>, and Serafina Arlotta. What it does is move the likely error from the
      <em>parents</em> to the <em>year</em> — and this archive has now caught the tree six years out
      on Salvatore Mazza, six on Orazio Mazza and <strong>eleven on Giovanni Arena</strong>. A Rocco
      Arena di Orazio in some other year is now a specific, findable thing rather than a hope.</p>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
      della restaurazione, Scilla, Nati 1859 — Antenati <code>an_ua2002928</code>, container
      <code>5vEYzey</code>, index at images 156–163. Read from the <strong>whole uncropped
      page</strong>: after the 1861 misalignment, this archive no longer reads parentage off a
      cropped index column, because on a full page you can see for yourself that the child's name and
      its parents sit on the same ruled line.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Scilla, Nati 1851 — the Bourbon index, which names the parents</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Michele Donato is not in the year the tree gives him</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The pre-unification <em>Tavola annuale alfabetica de' Nati</em> is the best-designed index in
      this archive's whole territory: <strong>Num. d'ordine · Cognomi e Nomi de' Nati · Patria ·
      Cognomi e Nomi de' Genitori · Giorno della Nascita</strong>. It <strong>names both
      parents</strong>, so a whole year's households can be read off one opening without opening a
      single act. For <P n="Michele Donato" /> — <P n="Nunnziata Donato" />'s father, who sailed from
      Palermo and landed at New Orleans on 11 March 1908 — it should have been one crop.</p>
      <p><strong>The D section of 1851 runs from entry 65 to entry 75, and there is no Donato in
      it.</strong> Eleven births: seven Di Franco, two Dormì, one De Marco, one De Alessandro. Entry
      64 is the last C and entry 76 begins the F, so the block is complete and nothing is hidden by a
      page turn.</p>
      <p><strong>The explanation is above.</strong> Act 162 of 1892 makes him thirty-six in July of
      that year, so born about <strong>1856</strong> — the archive was reading a volume five years
      too early. What follows was written before that act was found, and is left standing because the
      reasoning was right even while the conclusion was still open:</p>
      <p>Two explanations are open, and this archive has evidence for only one of them.
      <strong>The tree's birth years have been wrong before</strong> — six years out on Salvatore
      Mazza, six on Orazio Mazza, and, as of act 230 above, <strong>eleven years out on Giovanni
      Arena</strong>. His eldest recorded son was born in 1878, which puts his own birth anywhere from
      about 1848 to 1858. A wrong year is likelier than a wrong town, but that is a hypothesis and it
      is filed as one.</p>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">The same opening yields one further fact worth
      keeping: <strong>the 1851 A section holds a single Arena birth</strong> — Arena Maria, of
      Pasquale, at Scilla. The Arena of Scilla were not numerous in the 1850s.</p>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
      della restaurazione, Scilla, Nati 1851 — Antenati <code>an_ua2002901</code>, container
      <code>LeJQDD6</code>, index at images 123–131. <strong>These scans are small</strong> — about
      1000 × 1500 pixels, against 4600 × 3700 for the Italian-era volumes — which is why the IIIF
      request must ask for <code>/full/</code> and not a width, and why the 1861 index misled this
      archive earlier in the day.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The sibling sweep — Scilla, Nati 1852–1855</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Rocco Arena's brothers, and the custom landing exactly</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>With Rocco Arena reassigned to <strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong> and Serafina
      Arlotta, the obvious next question was how many other children that couple had — and the
      obvious place to look was the years the archive had not read. <strong>1852, 1853, 1854 and
      1855 are now read in full.</strong></p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:18px">
        <div class="k">1852 · atto 48</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Arena FILIPPO</strong> — <em>Ant.<sup>o</sup> e Arlotta Serafina</em>
        — 14 February</div><div class="w">the eldest</div>
        <div class="k">1853 · atto 2</div>
        <div class="v">Arena Domenico — <em>Francesant.<sup>o</sup> e Serafina Arlotta</em> —
        24 March</div><div class="w"></div>
        <div class="k">1860 · atto 45</div>
        <div class="v">Arena Giuseppe — <em>Franc. Ant.<sup>o</sup> e Arlotta Serafina</em> —
        5 March</div><div class="w"></div>
        <div class="k">1861 · atto 228</div>
        <div class="v"><P n="Rocco Arena" /> — <em>Francesco Antonio Arena, marinaro, e Serafina
        Arlotta</em> — 20 December</div><div class="w">the youngest</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>The first son is called Filippo — and Filippo is exactly
      who he should be.</strong> Act 186 of 1819 gives Francesco Antonio's own father as
      <strong>Filippo Arena</strong>. Calabrian custom names the first son for his father's father.
      The archive did not go looking for this; it fell out of a sweep run for a different reason,
      which is the strongest kind of confirmation there is.</p>
      <p>It also moves the marriage again. The couple were married by <strong>1851</strong> — which
      is the real reason they are absent from the Scilla marriage indexes of 1858 and 1859, and it
      retires the guess that Serafina was married in her own comune. <em>That</em> may still be true;
      it is simply no longer needed to explain anything.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><strong>One honest caveat on act 48.</strong> The 1852
      index writes the father as <em>"Ant.<sup>o</sup>"</em> where 1853 writes
      <em>"Francesant.<sup>o</sup>"</em>. So the identification rests on the surname plus the wife's
      distinctive name — <strong>Arlotta Serafina</strong> — and not on the father's forename. A
      different Antonio Arena married to a different Serafina Arlotta is not impossible, only
      unlikely.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">And Orazio's first child was a daughter</h3>
      <p>The same 1853 opening gives <strong>Arena Teresa, of <em>Orazio e Giov.<sup>a</sup>
      Zirilli</em>, 17 October</strong> — three years before the Domenico of 1856, and the earliest
      child of theirs the archive has found. 1852, 1854 and 1855 hold none.</p>
      <p><strong>That vindicates the retraction above</strong>: Domenico was not Orazio's first
      child, so no argument about first sons could ever have been run on him. And custom gives the
      first <em>daughter</em> her <strong>paternal grandmother's</strong> name — where act 10 of 1821
      makes Orazio's mother a <strong>Teresa</strong>.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><strong>It is still not settled, and here is the part
      that does not fit.</strong> If the 1821 Orazio is ours, his father was a <em>Giuseppe</em> —
      so his first son should be Giuseppe. Orazio's first son is <strong>Domenico</strong>, and 1852
      to 1855 are now read with no earlier son to rescue the point. So the daughter's name supports
      the 1821 candidate and the son's name argues against it. <strong>The archive is not going to
      pick whichever half suits it.</strong> Orazio Arena's own birth stays a candidate, not a
      finding.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Scilla, Nati 1852 — the acts are
      <code>an_ua2002904</code> (container <code>wX9avAO</code>) and the Tavola is bound separately as
      <code>an_ua2002907</code> (container <code>LNolBvO</code>, images 33–40), the same split as 1856
      and 1875. Nati 1853 <code>an_ua2002909</code>; 1854 <code>an_ua2002911</code>;
      1855 <code>an_ua2002914</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">A retraction, and what it teaches</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The archive withdraws its own inference about Orazio's father</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Earlier the same day this archive wrote, and labelled clearly as a lead rather than a
      finding: <em>"Calabrian naming custom gives the first son his father's father's name, and
      Orazio's first son is Domenico — twice. On that custom alone, Orazio Arena's father was a
      Domenico."</em></p>
      <p><strong>Two acts now say otherwise.</strong> Scilla has two Orazio Arena births in the
      right era:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
        <div class="k">1821 · atto 10</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Arena Orazio</strong> — <em>Gius.<sup>e</sup> e M[igliara?]
        Teresa</em> — 14 December</div><div class="w">matches the tree's year</div>
        <div class="k">1833 · atto 1</div>
        <div class="v">Arena Orazio — <em>Bonaventura e Romano Marianna</em> — 4 February</div>
        <div class="w">twelve years adrift</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Neither father is a Domenico.</strong> The 1821 act is
      much the better candidate — the family tree dates Orazio to <strong>1821</strong> and gives no
      source for it, and here is an Orazio Arena born 14 December 1821.</p>
      <p><strong>The instructive part is why the inference failed.</strong> It was not the custom
      that was wrong. It was the assumption underneath: that the first child <em>this archive had
      found</em> was Orazio's <em>first</em> child. The Scilla indexes for <strong>1850–1855 are
      unread</strong>. A son Giuseppe in any of them — named for a paternal grandfather Giuseppe,
      exactly as act 10 of 1821 would predict — would restore the custom perfectly and leave Domenico
      as the second son, named for Giovanna Zirilli's father.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><strong>The rule this leaves.</strong> Naming custom is
      only as good as your knowledge of the birth order, and a register read from 1856 onward cannot
      tell you the birth order of a couple who married before 1856. The inference should never have
      been made from a partial sweep — and it is written up here rather than quietly deleted, because
      the reasoning is the part worth keeping.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Scilla,
      Nati 1821 — <code>an_ua2002812</code>, container <code>04rZVeY</code>, Tavola at images 94–99;
      Nati 1833 — <code>an_ua2002847</code>, container <code>5B6RQPk</code>, Tavola at images 94–100.
      The 1821 mother's surname is read from a single index line and is not certain.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Three D sections, three years, no Donato</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The question changes shape</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Act 162 of 1892 puts <P n="Michele Donato" />'s birth at about <strong>1856</strong>. So the
      1856 Tavola was opened, and then 1859 for good measure. <strong>Neither has a Donato in it,
      and neither did 1851.</strong> Each D block is complete — bounded by the last C above and the
      first F below — so nothing is hidden by a page turn:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
        <div class="k">1851</div>
        <div class="v">entries 65–75 — seven Di Franco, two Dormì, one De Marco, one
        De Alessandro</div><div class="w">no Donato</div>
        <div class="k">1856</div>
        <div class="v">entries 24–235 — Denisi, six Difranco, two Dormì, D'Elia, De Nava,
        De Marco</div><div class="w">no Donato</div>
        <div class="k">1859</div>
        <div class="v">entries 73–275 — six De Franco, two Dormì, De Ciccio, De Elia,
        D'Ignoto</div><div class="w">no Donato</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Three years spanning the whole window his daughter's act
      allows, and the surname is simply not there.</strong> Meanwhile the 1892 index has
      <em>two</em> Donato households — <strong>Donato Annunziata di Michele</strong> at act 162 and
      <strong>Donato Domenico di Rocco</strong> at act 38.</p>
      <p>That is a different question from the one this archive started with. It is no longer
      <em>"which year was Michele Donato born at Scilla?"</em> but <strong>"when did the Donato come
      to Scilla, and from where?"</strong> — because on this evidence the surname arrives somewhere
      between 1859 and 1892. Michele was very likely <strong>born in another comune</strong> and
      settled here; his sailing from <strong>Palermo</strong> to New Orleans in 1908 is at least
      consistent with a family that moved.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>This is three sampled years, not a proof.</strong>
      1852–55, 1857, 1858 and 1860–65 are unread, and the surname could be sitting in any of them.
      But a negative that repeats across a spread of years is worth much more than one that does not,
      and it is the reason this archive will now look at his <strong>marriage</strong> rather than
      his birth: a marriage act names the groom's <em>comune of birth</em> outright.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Scilla, Matrimoni 1913 — the whole index</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Fifty-eight marriages, and four surnames that are not among them</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Antenati's Scilla marriage series runs <strong>1905–1913 only</strong>. The 1913 volume's
      container was already in hand, and — unlike the portal's HTML, which rate-limits after about
      three page loads — <strong>the IIIF images are not throttled at all</strong>. So all eleven
      index openings came down for nothing, and the year is transcribed here in full.</p>
      <p>The reason for opening it was <a href={u("/people/giovanni-polistena/")}>Giovanni
      Polistena</a>'s marriage to <strong>Nunziata Donato</strong>, which would name four of
      <P n="Angela Polistena" />'s grandparents in a single act. <strong>It is not here.</strong>
      Neither is any Arena, any Donato, or any Mazza:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
        {Object.entries(m13.wanted).map(([k, v]) => (
          <>
            <div class="k">{k}</div>
            <div class="v">{v === 0
              ? <>absent — <strong>no {k} married at Scilla in 1913</strong></>
              : <>{v} {v === 1 ? "marriage" : "marriages"}</>}</div>
            <div class="w">{v === 0 ? "closes a search" : "of 58"}</div>
          </>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>A negative result is still a result</strong>, and this one
      is cheap to state and expensive to re-derive. The Polistena–Donato marriage is either outside
      1905–13, or it was not celebrated at Scilla. Eight volumes remain in the window; this archive
      has done one.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.8rem">The surname distribution is its own small finding. Fifty-eight
      couples produced <strong>{m13.surnames.length} surnames</strong>, and the commonest of them —
      {m13.surnames.slice(0, 6).map(([n, c], i) => <>{i > 0 && ", "}<strong>{n}</strong> ({c})</>)} —
      is the population the Arena, Polistena and Donato households were marrying into.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:20px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Groom</th><th>of</th><th>Bride</th><th>of</th><th class="m">Date</th><th class="m">Act</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {m13.rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{r.groom}</td><td class="small">{r.groomFather}</td>
              <td>{r.bride}</td><td class="small">{r.brideFather}</td>
              <td class="m">{r.date}</td>
              <td class="m">{r.act}{r.parte !== "—" && r.parte !== "I" ? " · " + r.parte : ""}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px">One entry was worth opening the act for. Index no. 32 read
    as a possible <strong>Mazza</strong> bride — which would have put the Sicilian surname in Scilla,
    a genuinely surprising thing. <strong>Act 31 says <em>Muzzi</em>.</strong> Majori Carmelo, 27, a
    carpenter born at Gallico, married <strong>Muzzi Santa Maria</strong>, 24, of Scilla, on
    20 September 1913. Read the act, not the index.</p>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:14px">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
    italiano, Scilla, Matrimoni 1913 — Antenati <code>an_ua16594965</code>, container
    <code>5K7arD3</code>, index at images 75–85.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">A correction, in the open</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The index said something else, and the caution caught it</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Reading the 1861 index, this archive recorded Rocco Arena's parents as
      <strong>«Giuseppe e Zirilli Grazia»</strong> — and noted that while the rare surname
      <em>Zirilli</em> agreed with the family tree's <em>Giovana Zirilli</em>, both forenames did not.
      It was filed as a <strong>candidate, not a join</strong>, with the words <em>"read the act
      itself before joining"</em>.</p>
      <p><strong>The act says Francesco Antonio Arena and Serafina Arlotta.</strong> The index's
      parents column had been misaligned by one row: it is a wide ruled table photographed across a
      gutter, and once the columns are cropped apart the row a name sits on is not reliably the row
      its parents sit on.</p>
      <p><strong>That caution is the only reason the archive does not now carry a wrong set of
      great-great-grandparents.</strong> Had the surname agreement been treated as sufficient — as
      the <a href={u("/register-1879/")}>Venerio reading</a> was, earlier the same day — it would have
      been merged and published.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">And an unresolved conflict</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">This is not the tree's Rocco Arena</h2>
    <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
      <div class="k">The tree says</div>
      <div class="v">Rocco Arena, b. 1861, son of <strong>Orazio Arena</strong> and
      <strong>Giovana Zirilli</strong></div><div class="w">no source</div>
      <div class="k">The register says</div>
      <div class="v">Rocco Arena, b. <strong>20 December 1861</strong>, son of
      <strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong>, marinaro, and <strong>Serafina Arlotta</strong></div>
      <div class="w">atto 228</div>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px">The 1861 Scilla register contains <strong>exactly one
    Rocco Arena</strong>, and <strong>neither parent matches</strong>. So either the tree's parents
    are wrong, or its birth year is. This archive has already found the same tree
    <strong>six years out</strong> on Salvatore Mazza's birth and six on Orazio Mazza's, so the year
    is the likelier error — but that is a hypothesis, not a finding, and the Arena line stays where it
    was until a record settles it. <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>The queue →</a></p>
  </section>

